development.question_text:
A graph of $y = \sec x$ is drawn on the domain $[-\pi, \pi]$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
  xmin=-3.5, xmax=3.5, ymin=-4, ymax=4,
  xtick={-3.1416, -1.5708, 1.5708, 3.1416},
  xticklabels={$-\pi$, $-\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\frac{\pi}{2}$, $\pi$},
  ytick={-3,-2,-1,1,2,3},
  samples=150, clip=false,
  restrict y to domain=-4:4, unbounded coords=jump,
  axis line style={-{Stealth}}, width=10cm, height=6.5cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=-3.1416:-1.83] {1/cos(deg(x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=-1.31:1.31] {1/cos(deg(x))};
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, domain=1.83:3.1416] {1/cos(deg(x))};
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:4] ({-1.5708},{x});
\addplot[dashed, gray, domain=-4:4] ({1.5708},{x});
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Using the graph, state the coordinates of the $y$-intercept and all local extrema on $[-\pi, \pi]$.
